WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
John: Good morning Hank, It's Tuesday.

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:05.180
So on Friday, Rosianna and I left the magic
and awesome of NerdCon: Stories to head up

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.050
to Princeton, Minnesota, where I raced cars
on a dirt track against best-selling author

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:11.410
and all around awesome person Maggie Stiefvater.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:15.099
So Hank, an IndyCar driver I know once told
me that in racing the only thing more dangerous

00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:18.190
than being too aggressive is being too cautious.

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:21.990
Which is an observation that I think applies
to more than just racing, and while I'm certainly

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:26.510
an uncommonly anxious person, I don't think
of myself as an overly cautious one.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:29.590
So I was feeling kinda excited as we arrived
at the Princeton Speedway.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:32.720
I mean at no point did I actually think I
could win the race but I did think I could,

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.520
like, finish it without being pulled out of
a burning vehicle which as it turned out was

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:37.550
overly optimistic.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:41.430
Took my first ever ride in a four-wheeler
to my race car which belonged to a 17-year-old,

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:42.430
hence its number.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:44.410
My coach for the evening was his dad, Alan.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.980
And special shoutout to Alan's kids, my pit
crew, ten year old Allison and eight year

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:48.980
old Aaron.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.690
My first lesson was getting in and out of
the car, which as you can see is ridiculously

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:53.690
difficult.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:57.149
Hank, I need to linger on this shot for a
moment because I want you to understand how

00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:01.329
hard it is to get out of these cars even when
they are not on fire.

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:05.880
Also, I want you to notice my excellent Eleanor
and Park t-shirt, which I got at dftba.com.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.570
So Alan's an experienced race car driver and
an excellent coach.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:10.130
Alan: You don't want to jump up on the throttle...

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:13.889
John: He explained to me at least a dozen
times that coming out of the corner, I would

00:01:13.889 --> 00:01:17.520
feel an urge to over-correct the steering
and/or let off the throttle and that giving

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.500
into that urge would be a significant mistake
and I was able to remember that advice for,

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.829
like, five-and-a-half laps of actual racing.

00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:28.429
Put on my smoking hot fire suit, my helmet,
a pair of gloves, and then Maggie showed up

00:01:28.429 --> 00:01:30.509
with her awesome car with its awesome license
plate.

00:01:30.509 --> 00:01:34.329
And then after an impossibly beautiful sunset
came to Princeton, I belted myself in for

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:36.490
our first of three scheduled races.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:39.841
Maggie jumped out to a lead, and she was faster
than me and also a much better driver, but

00:01:39.841 --> 00:01:44.060
my car turned really well in the dirt and
its power was exciting and intoxicating and

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.719
I was just trying to keep up with her but
then I thought, "You know, maybe I could drive

00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:49.509
alongside of her and really make a race out
of this."

00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:52.719
And then on the fifth lap coming out of turn
four, I felt an urge to correct the steering

00:01:52.719 --> 00:01:54.259
and yeah...

00:01:54.259 --> 00:01:58.530
The speedway staff got to me very quickly
and although I could feel the heat of the

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:04.929
fire in my back and legs, they kept me calm
and pulled me out and I was fine, but I'm

00:02:04.929 --> 00:02:05.929
not going to lie, Hank, it was terrifying.

00:02:05.929 --> 00:02:09.849
But then they fixed the problem with the car
and for some reason, I decided to get back

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:10.849
in it.

00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:14.930
In fact, I won the second heat - more precisely,
Maggie let me win - and I got a trophy and

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:19.120
everything and then we raced for a third time
and Maggie dominated me so thoroughly that

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:24.950
the regular pro-drivers at the speedway asked
to take her on and she beat them as well!

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:28.390
So Maggie won the race, but I caught fire
and didn't get hurt, which felt very much

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:29.689
like, you know, some kind of winning.

00:02:29.689 --> 00:02:33.590
And late that night as I tried to sleep, I
kept playing the race back in my mind: me

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:37.900
doing precisely the thing I'd been told not
to, the heat of the flames against my back,

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.150
my panicked hands as I tried to unbuckle the
belts, the smoky relief of being pulled from

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.150
the car.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:44.480
There are of course smart risks.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.780
The proper line to run through life is somewhere
between extreme caution and extreme aggression,

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.400
but this wasn't one of them.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.390
That said, Hank, I have to confess that I
am proud to have lost to Maggie, but not to

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:55.390
myself.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:57.780
But in the end, Hank, I think I'm going to
use this occasion to announce my retirement

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:58.859
from race car driving.

00:02:58.859 --> 00:03:01.629
I'm headed back to the grandstands where I
belong.

00:03:01.629 --> 00:03:02.959
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

